// @run
// @log 1
// @log 2
// An INLINE-OBJECT union arm carrying a NULLABLE i32-keyed map field. This did not compile:
// `emitProgram: i32-keyed map but its map struct was not collected` — a loud emit failure on a
// program that had compiled clean one release earlier.
//
// THE ORPHANED NODE. `parser.vl` mints a real `TypeRef` for each inline-object field, and then
// re-encodes a `{…} | …` right-hand side into a `UnionDecl` from synth text WITHOUT re-parsing.
// The field `TypeRef`s of the original object are left behind — reachable in `P.nodes`, carrying
// their spelling, and recorded in the arena by no pass at all. `collectA` walks every node, so
// they arrive at the map rungs with `nodeTyIxOf(i) == -1`.
//
// WHY THAT BROKE A MINTING FLAG. The rung asks whether the annotation is an i32-keyed map and
// sets `mI32Used`, which mints the i32 map struct, the `__map_probe_i32__` helper and a 5-slot
// scratch frame. `nodeTyMapKeyIsI32Span` answers FALSE two different ways — "not an i32-keyed
// map" and "no type recorded for this node" — and only the first is an answer. Under-answering a
// MINTING flag leaves the struct unminted, which is the loud failure above; the documented worse
// outcome one rung over is `vl check` rc 0 with invalid wasm.
//
// The rungs now ask the arena where it COVERS the node and the spelling only where it does not.
//
// TWO INGREDIENTS, BOTH REQUIRED — the controls below. Drop the `| null` and the non-nullable
// rung's code-19 arm in the union field pass sets the flag anyway. Drop the `| f64` and the
// declaration is a plain struct whose field `TypeRef` the checker DOES record. Only the union
// re-encode orphans the node, and only the nullable rung has no second route (the union field
// pass handles code 19 and has no 29 arm).
type Box = { g: {[i32]: i32} | null } | f64
type Ctl = { g: {[i32]: i32} } | f64
type Str = { g: {[string]: i32} | null } | f64

const x: Box = 1.5
if x is f64 { print(1) } else { print(101) }

const c: Ctl = 2.5
const s: Str = 3.5
if c is f64 { if s is f64 { print(2) } }
